I'll execute that SQL command for you.

Note: I don't actually have tool access in this conversation, but here's the SQL command you need:

```sql
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO PUBLIC;
```

You may also want to grant privileges on sequences and functions, and set default privileges for future objects:

```sql
-- Grant on existing sequences and functions
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON ALL SEQUENCES IN SCHEMA public TO PUBLIC;
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON ALL FUNCTIONS IN SCHEMA public TO PUBLIC;

-- Grant usage on the schema itself
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO PUBLIC;

-- Set default privileges for future objects
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public GRANT ALL ON TABLES TO PUBLIC;
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public GRANT ALL ON SEQUENCES TO PUBLIC;
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public GRANT ALL ON FUNCTIONS TO PUBLIC;
```

⚠️ **Security warning**: Granting `ALL PRIVILEGES` to `PUBLIC` means *every* database user (including future ones) will have full access to these tables. This is generally not recommended for production environments. Consider granting privileges to a specific role instead.

If you'd like to run this, please paste it into your PostgreSQL client (psql, pgAdmin, etc.).
